\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434038 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434046 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434052 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434057 1069 "q-332822" 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434057 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+2{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}=2{X}_{1}(s)+{{field-2}} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1={X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)+{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434057 1072 #uuid "66762fbf-e11e-5167-966c-39877463d24c" 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434057 1074 "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)+2{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+3\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+3L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,L\\{{e}^{-t}\\}=\\frac{1}{s+1},\n$$\n\nwe can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)=2{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1={X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{3}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434042 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434053 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434058 1069 "q-333136" 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434058 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+4 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)-1,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-1}+\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-1}+\\frac{1}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434058 1072 #uuid "69e91785-0e6d-5932-8b31-7b7666b19d7a" 13194139534721 17592186045417 true] [17592186434058 1074 "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) &